    reviewScore: 38,
    relevanceScore: 88,
    text: "After the mess that was the launch of No Man\u0027s Sky I think it deserved the response it received. We were promised many things, many of the things we were promised were not in the game we paid $60 for. Hello Games (especially Sean Murray) have been dragged through the mud since release. They could have taken every single cent from the huge amount of sales the game made and ran, but THEY didn\u0027t. From the rumors of Hello Games offices being abandoned to the MONTHS of silence from Hello Games . They\u0027ve come through, and from my understanding of Sean\u0027s GDC talk they simply ran out of money. NMS is an incredibly ambitious title for such a small team to create. I won\u0027t excuse their handling of the situation but in reality, even if they had told everyone that they ran out of money there would have been an uproar. Not nearly as big but none the less...an uproar.   I bought this game, I told my friends to pre-order. Non of us refunded it as we all had spent too long to be in the window but while we were stuck with this game we accepted what it was and that we were essentially out $60. Then, the first update came. It wasn\u0027t much but it was a glimmer of hope that Hello Games cared enough about this game to at least salvage it. Each subsequent update made that glimmer bright and brighter. With Atlas Rises I don\u0027t think anyone can logically question that Hello Games at least has the integrity to push through this dark, and dreary year they\u0027ve had to see this passion project through. I\u0027ve put at least 15hrs in since the Atlas Rises update and I\u0027ve barely scratched the surface of what this update has brought.   Is it worth $60? Did the version we were shown back before release look worth $60? If so then I would say yes. It is now 96% of the way towards being the game we were shown. (still no giant sand worms Sean!) The game we all fell in love with, the game we all spent our money on. The game Sean Murray teased and teased only to let us down for reasons we may never have the complete answers to. It\u0027s right there, in front of us all now. It\u0027s been a year since the implosion of the hype trains core. We\u0027ve had time to settle, Hello Games has been rebuilding and damn it. They\u0027ve rebuilt.",
