    reviewScore: 45,
    relevanceScore: 88,
    text: "CAVEAT: I waited and started the game after 1.33, which is a whole new game from version 1.0.  This game is simply incredible! This is above and beyond what I had wanted out of ANY space exploration game, and certainly 100x better than the initial release version. I had a day off and I put 9 hours into the game in one day (and that was all on the starting planet, after I received my first spaceship, and found a crashed/damaged Hauler which I proceeded to fix-up).   Right now, I have a cumulative of 13.3 hours into the experience, and I don\u0027t have a vast majority of the content or story unlocked. I am aware of the state the game came-out in and what was missing, but after reading the patch notes of the last 3 patches (really should be referred to as MASSIVE expansions!... did I mention they were free?!), the game is now a LOT deeper than it was.   I really wanted to get this game on pre-order, but my gut told me it may not live-up to the hype... and it didn\u0027t. After a MASSIVE over-haul, and when it was on sale for 66% off, it was a no-brainer for me to get this thing finally.  One of the early complaints I saw were about how people got bored with the game and just blazed through the (at the time) limited story narrative with nothing to achieve... I started my first game in Survival mode. I highly recommend it, as it is exactly as advertised. Death came early to me while I was learning that I could not breath the toxic atmosphere of my first planet and that I had to gather resources, dart from cave to cave, and look for bases and other points of interest rather than just running willy-nilly to my main objective and getting off the planet.  There were TONS of Points of Interest on my starting planet, and they were a pleasure to look for. Resources were available, but definitely scarce, so survival definitely played a huge part in the exploration even after getting a ridiculously great Class A Multitool by pure random chance from one of the aliens I encountered early-on. Sure, you could fly anywhere quickly, but would you have access to enough Plutonium to power your thrusters to get up in the air again? Most of the time the answer was \u0027no\u0027... I had to venture through caves and explore minutes away from my spacecraft to gather what I needed. The wildlife was passive (thankfully), but the atmosphere was always choking me.  Going off planet to a hot moon for the first time was even more abusive (in a great way)... I was greeted by a fast howling creature which resulted in at least 3 deaths until I figured-out what the heck it was... and it was mean... and made me jump when I heard the scurrying sounds through my surround sound in the middle of the night. I would jump on top of my ship as soon as I would land just to scout the surroundings. It was a legit Aliens-type feeling, especially since the small moon was densely populated with other wild life which was constantly being spooked... and for good reason with that six-legged bas**** running around! Resources were even scarcer than on my first planet, and after several reloads (after I figured-out that I could reload saves \u003e_\u003c!), I managed to escape with next to no resources (which I felt were adequately stock-piled from the first planet), 1 health unit, literally drained Life Support, and no shields/resistance to the elements.  Next planet was in the new system and it was a beautiful Paradise planet with vast oceans and green islands... except it was crawling with ANGRY Sentinels that apparently destroyed all life... I was forced to run multiple times. Ultimately learned how to deal with them after a couple of near-deaths, and started farming as many as I could from a different location to net me some great loot. I have no idea how to survive going back to the central point crawling with Sentinels on this planet, but I gladly try! :)  I am pleased to announce that in Survival Mode, the game difficulty being \u0027too easy\u0027 is no longer an issue. There are multiple ways of farming money, and everything can kill you, but you always have a chance once you learn the basics. The game is difficult, but not brutal. I got a feeling of playing S.T.A.L.K.E.R. in space on more than a few occasions as I explored abandoned buildings, way points, and crash sites... it was wonderful!  I saw what the graphics looked like before, and I have to say that they look incredible at 1440p and a 980ti now. I have the FPS cap set to 60fps with VSYNC on, and I feel like the game is smooth but may occasionally dip, as judged by moments when it the game runs like butter. It is a minor note, as the game plays very consistently, and I can\u0027t tell if maybe the frame drops are in my head or not.  I did find a base with an Architect I could claim on the first planet, but have so far decided not to build my first base prior to unlocking some more Blueprints and greater access to credits and resources. So yeah, I have not even touched on one of the largest aspects of the game, not to mention the freighters! All I know is that I landed on the first Class C Freighter I saw, and the captain offered to sell it to me for 192 million credits... I had about 200K at the time.  So at this point I am GREATLY looking forward to spending more time with this game, while my girlfriend is terrified that I won\u0027t see here ever again... well, for at least the next 100 hours or so. ;)  Pros: - Great graphics - Fairly easy to figure everything out, so the learning curve is not particularly steep... if you know how to play FPS games and may have played a Survival-type game, you will probably do well. - Really great Survival Mode, which should be the default in my opinion - Tons to explore - Vastly improved planet types and creatures over the original - New progression system - Interesting economy mechanics - Really fun exponential growth model that REALLY lets you feel like you are starting from nothing and growing into a force to be reckoned with - Faction system that gives you additional unlocks - Great spaceship variety (in terms of visuals, however I have seen pretty interesting stat and slot combinations also... this is always going to be subjective, though) - Sound design gives weapons weight and startling moments when something sneaks-up on you (if you have surround sound) - There are vehicles now, too?! O_O - Plans for even more content, while the current build is already amazing!  Cons: - Not a lot of details on how to actually survive when you first start... I had no idea caves could protect me from toxic gas, which is the exact opposite of what I would personally expect (greater gas in cave pockets, not less o_O... I can live with it, though). - No explanation of the Quick Menu and all of the cool things you can do with it... pretty easy to use and figure-out at least. - Still some optimization work may help performance on older machines and fix the odd crash (I only had 1 when I loaded a prior save for the first time), but still highly playable.  Conclusion: The game has evolved greatly from its initial release, and this is exactly the type of game I wanted for the longest time. It seems to do things in an simpler/easier way than games like Elite Dangerous, but at the same time it does a lot of things better, and maybe a handful of things worse. It\u0027s a must buy for any lovers of space and survival genres, especially if you are looking for a great single-player experience as opposed to having to be surrounded by people in typically MMO\u0027s. You are truly a lost traveler and it feels like it in this world. I feel like it\u0027s finally worth full price, but it\u0027s a complete steal if you get it on sale! Two thumbs up! :)",
