    reviewScore: 94,
    relevanceScore: 88,
    text: "In short - the thousands of existing reviews aren\u0027t wrong. Knowing the reputation it had, I still had some interest in No Man\u0027s Sky and I finally got NMS on sale around the time of the 1.3 update. Right off the bat the game would crash at the loading screen. I thought maybe it was a graphics or configuration setting. Then I noticed you aren\u0027t given the option to make any configuration changes... There\u0027s no main menu, only a difficulty select screen. So I never would have known if that was the problem. Having to load the game before you can change graphics settings? That\u0027s a horrible design choice. After a quick internet search I found out that NMS has a known issue with my specific series of AMD graphics... So, in my frustration and research I found that NMS allows mods, and THANK THE INTERNET GODS FOR NOMANSSKYMODS dot COM!!!! One of the top listed mods was a game fix for my graphics card. Loaded it up and the game was working! After seeing trailers and other gameplay footage I was excited to see my starting planet - which was a barren rock. I quickly picked up the primary game play mechanics and started on the missions of repairing my stuff. That\u0027s where I quickly hit a roadblock. I needed \u0027Heridium\u0027 to repair my ship. I ventured out into a dangerous, freezing and barren planet walking towards every Waypoint in sight. I found nothing. I walked and scanned finding Carbon and Iron, Carbon and Iron, Carbon and Iron and nothing else. I looked online, thinking maybe I was missing something obvious or doing something wrong, but no - there was no Heridium to be found. So finally, after TWO HOURS of walking and scanning (occassionally hiding in a cave from the cold) I found Heridium. I mined all that I could carry, which is where I hit my next roadblock. I had to dedicate 2 more hours of walking back to my ship, while maintaining enough resources to keep my suit\u0027s life support online and having almost no inventory slots. This was a horrible and tedious process and if I hadn\u0027t already dedicated so much time to the game I would have just deleted the save and started over. So, after 4 hours I was able to repair my ship and actually start the \"game.\" I took a break to watch some other players on YouTube and didn\u0027t find a single person who had the terrible luck I had. Most people had their ships repaired within 20 minutes or less even on their first plays. I immediately jumped to the next closest planet, knowing it couldn\u0027t be as bad as the planet I just left... and I was wrong. The next planet didn\u0027t even have enough Carbon for me to keep mining! No Carbon, no Iron and I could barely scrape together enough resources to launch my ships thrusters again so I could escape that hell. My confidence was pretty broken at that point. From, then I was playing a couple hours every night just to try and find the amazing planets and creatures I had seen in screenshots and videos. Occasionally I would break away from searching planets to follow the story line but the story seemed to take the \"anything could be anything - it\u0027s so mysterious\" approach and I wasn\u0027t into it. After 22 hours I finally found an interesting planet, with beaches and grass and creatures! Then I discovered I couldn\u0027t set a marker or any indicator to find that planet again. So, going back to the internet I found out that you can get the ability to set your own Waypoint IF you build a base. So could I build a base on this planet? No. So I warped around more planets until I found a place I could build a base... And of course it was on a hostile planet full of aggressive creatures and extreme heat. I did all of the grinding it requires to build it up so I could build a Waypoint, got VERY lucky and managed to find the \u0027good\u0027 planet again and setup a marker but by that time I was pretty burnt out on the grinding this game requires for progression. I will continue playing to finish the story but overall I couldn\u0027t recommend this game, based on the experience I\u0027ve had. I give credit to Hello Games for what they have done and I think NMS is an amazing feat but I think a tighter, more focused experience would have made us all happier. It is my hope that they will develop a spin-off game with a small universe and a better focus on story telling. The game is beautiful and has some amazing moments sprinkled in, but those feel like a couple of strawberries thrown on top of the largest stale sheet-cake ever created.  Edit: The above experience was on the \"Normal\" difficulty setting. I did not even attempt other settings until much later.",
