    relevanceScore: 84,
    text: "It\u0027s no secret that this game was panned as absolute trash when it came out. The same old cycle of gameplay from the second hour through to the last with nothing much else to do. Hello Games was rightly flamed for what they released and the blatent lies their founder made, however in the past year it\u0027s clear, even to a newer player like me, that the developers want to clear their name and give those that trusted them with their money a game worthy of that pricetag. I\u0027m not going to say this game is perfect and it\u0027s certainly not for everyone, but it\u0027s clear it\u0027s come a long way since that disastrous launch.  Patch 1.3 adds some much-needed depth to the game, with an engaging storyline and many great tweaks and UI improvements. This was on top of a pretty fun and interesting Base-building sidestory introduced in 1.1.  As for the base game itself, don\u0027t expect an action-adventure. The game, for the most part, leaves you to your own devices when it comes to what you want to do. There\u0027s generally two main paths you can take from the start, along with the new story and the basebuilding series. On the other hand you can choose to complete missions for rep with the three races and three guilds. Or you could choose to mine some expencive metals and jump from system to system selling your wares. Or you could become a pirate, destroying other ships for their cargo.  If you played at release and were frustrated by the gameplay, now is the best time so far to come back and check it out again. If you\u0027re looking at buying this game for the first time and you\u0027re looking for a laid-back, chill, not too difficult exploration game I can recommend it at this point.  Hello games hasn\u0027t redeemed themselves for the game they initially released, but their consistant, high-quality patches and the amount of time they\u0027ve spent post-release, making this game what it should\u0027ve been from the start is commendable. After all, it\u0027s refreshing to see this sort of thing in an age of moneygrabbing DLC passes, yearly rehashes, lousy ports, rereleases, Pay To Win and effectively abandoned Early Access titles.",
