    sentiment: 'positive',
    helpful: 7,
    funny: 9,
    createdAt: '2017-08-11',
    reviewScore: 81,
    relevanceScore: 83,
    text: "You might be surprised at some of the things I don\u0027t regret doing. One of them was buying this game at launch at full price. Whether it had to be released by a deadline according to a contract, or because the development team ran out of money and had to release [i]something[/i] to keep from starving, the game as it was caused . . . several . . . people to raise the perfectly valid complaint that the game felt unfinished. Some even leveled the accusation, now all but proven false, that the development team were greedy liars. If the developers had enough time to make and release the game as it exists today, no one would have complained. If today\u0027s No Man\u0027s Sky was the first release of No Man\u0027s Sky, the mountain of negative reviews on this page would not exist. If this was the first version of the game the world at large knew, it would have been praised by perhaps not all, but a substantial portion of the haters. Now, obviously, that\u0027s not what happened, and the existence of previous versions is now a canon part of the lore, but you shouldn\u0027t judge a game by what it was, you should judge it by what it is. No Man\u0027s Sky is beautiful. No Man\u0027s Sky is thrilling. No Man\u0027s Sky is compelling. No Man\u0027s Sky is just a little scary now and then. No Man\u0027s Sky is complete. But that\u0027s not to say No Man\u0027s Sky is over, not by a long shot. No Man\u0027s Sky is back, and it\u0027s here to stay.",
