    text: "[Update Dec 2018, NEXT and stuff...]  Update has excellent multiplayer features, dogfighting, combat, missions, quests, basebuilding, crafting, exploring, grinding, mining, and is a lot of fun.  My only complaint is lack of joystick support for the excellent space combat, and progress seems to really hit a wall and slow down a lot at a certain point, when there\u0027s still so much cool stuff you could build or buy that seems out of reach. Certain critical resources are very hard to find, and in other cases, prices are very expensive compared to how much you\u0027re bringing in. Maybe it\u0027s just a matter of \"git gud.\"  I liked this game before. It\u0027s improved a lot since then, I still heartily give it a thumbs up. 4/5. If progression was a little more straightforward and/or smoother, and it had joystick support, it would be a 5.  [Previous Review Below...]  I was one of the ones who stayed on the sidelines while this game was being developed, then when it was released to mixed or negative reviews. I don\u0027t know how bad it was before, but I can say it\u0027s quite a bit of fun now. There\u0027s a steady sense of progression from the very first moment. As you make your way around your starting planet, then to other planets, then to other systems and space stations, the game places objectives before you to help lead you towards more and more self-reliance. Maybe this game gets bad after x number of hours, but after 4.5 hours it\u0027s still nice and intriguing. There\u0027s a mystery to solve, ancient structures to explore, and as you do so you continue to unlock more technology to help along the way.  Technically the game is fine. I\u0027m running on Win7 x64 with an i7-6700k, 32MB RAM, and GTX 980 video card. I get a solid 60FPS everywhere, but I\u0027m never super aggressive on graphics settings. Give me high res textures with anisotropic filtering and I\u0027m happy. This game has pretty extensive quality settings, so you\u0027re sure to be able to tune it to your system with no problems.  I only have two minor complaints so far about the gameplay. First, the inventory is very limited, and the stack sizes are relatively small compared to how much material you\u0027ll need for your journey. For this reason you end up spending quite a bit of time gathering resources and juggling inventory. This was one of the initial complaints and it\u0027s still a bit annoying. There are ways to upgrade your personal inventory capacity, and you will eventually find bigger ships. You can transfer inventory directly to your ship from anywhere. After about 3-4 hours I did find a ship that was able to hold twice as much cargo, but almost all the slots are locked and require quite a few credits to unlock.  My other complaint, and this is purely a matter of personal preference, is that the universe is rather lonely. There are NPCs to interact with, but so far I haven\u0027t been forming any relationships, and they\u0027re all very alien in appearance. I like games with NPC companions, like Mass Effect and the like, but this game just has you against a mostly hostile or indifferent universe. There is an NPC that shows up for regular appearances as a matter of story, but I wouldn\u0027t call him/her a companion by any means. Being able to form a crew and having RPG options wouldn\u0027t go amiss in my book, but that can hardly be a mark against this title.  Travel times are pretty acceptable, with four different speed options - cruising, which requires you to constantly hold your thrust button down, a boost speed which is kind of like afterburners, but it lasts as long as you have fuel, a phase drive which is kind of a sublight speed, and then warp drive, which almost instantly takes you from one star system to the next. Going from space to atmosphere, then down to the surface and back is a smooth process with no hitching or jarring transitions. If your angle is too steep, you\u0027ll get a burning aurora effect around the edges of your viewable area, along with some shaking and creaking sounds, which is a cool touch. So far it appears to be visual only.  You also get a scanner, which can be upgraded. At first it will detect materials and points of interest within a certain radius. If you aim at a planet you can scan it and see what conditions to expect, and what kinds of materials are most abundant there. All planets I\u0027ve found so far have the essentials - iron, carbon, and plutonium, which you need in order to maintain your various energy levels and conduct basic repairs. It\u0027s the more exotic materials you\u0027ll see as the result of a long distance scan.  In my time of playing I have never found a planet that wasn\u0027t hostile enough to drain your environmental systems while you\u0027re on foot. This system is like a hunger or thirst meter that runs down constantly. Sometimes there will be storms or something that makes it go down even faster. This means you can\u0027t venture out too far from your ship or the nearest shelter without either going back to let your bar fill back up again, or using some of the materials you\u0027ve picked up to refill it out in the field. I\u0027m sure there are M class planets around somewhere but I\u0027ve yet to discover any.  Overall this is a pretty decent game. It\u0027s good enough that I can see putting 20-40+ hours into it at least. We\u0027ll just have to see beyond that. I haven\u0027t tried the new multiplayer features, but if that system is developed enough it might be a blast to do some base building with friends or family. We\u0027ll see. So far though, it\u0027s a pretty solid game, easily worth picking up on sale at least. The current sale price of $24 seems like a pretty good bargain for something like this, but I don\u0027t know if I\u0027d go much higher than that.",
